  "question": {
    "text": "The <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> bond length is maximum in:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>70</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "graphite",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "diamond",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "In diamond, all carbons are <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> hybridised and hence bond lengths are similar to carbon-carbon single bonds. <br/>Graphite, <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>70</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow></msub></math> forms of carbon have delocalisation of electrons which leads to double bond characteristics. Hence, their bond lengths are in between carbon-carbon single and double bonds. <br/>Hence, diamond with only single bond character will be have the longest bond."
  }
}